== Chapter VII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[General rules without which no lust will be mortified — No mortification unless a man is a believer — The dangers of unregenerate persons attempting to mortify sin — The duty of unconverted persons in this matter of mortification considered — The emptiness of the Papists’ attempts and rules for mortification shown from this.]

=== II. General Rules

#smallcaps[Next] we come to consider the #emph[ways] and #emph[means] by which a soul may go about the mortification of any particular lust and sin that Satan takes advantage of to disquiet and weaken him.

But first some general considerations must be laid down about certain principles and foundations of this work. Without them no man in the world can attain to the mortification of any sin, however much he is stirred by convictions and resolved to mortify it.

These are the general rules and principles without which no sin will ever be mortified:

==== First Rule: Only a Believer Can Mortify Sin

#emph[Unless a man is a believer, that is, one truly] grafted into Christ#emph[, he can never mortify a single sin]. I do not say unless he knows himself to be one, but unless he really is one.

Mortification is the work of believers: “if by the Spirit you#super[+] put to death,” and so on (Rom 8:13); you#super[+] #emph[believers], for whom there is no condemnation (verse 1). They alone are urged to it: “Put to death, therefore, the components of your earthly nature” (Col 3:5). Who is to mortify? You#super[+] who “have been raised with Christ” (verse 1), whose “life is now hidden with Christ in God” (verse 3), who “will appear with Him in glory” (verse 4). An unregenerate man may do something like it, but the work itself, in a way acceptable to God, he can never perform. You#super[+] know what a picture of it some of the philosophers drew: Seneca, Cicero, Epictetus. What moving discourses they have about contempt of the world and of self, and about governing and conquering all excessive affections and passions! Yet the lives of most of them showed that their maxims differed as much from true mortification as the sun painted on a signpost differs from the sun in the sky; they had neither light nor heat. Their own Lucian shows well enough what they all were. There is no death of sin without the death of Christ. You#super[+] know what attempts at it are made by the Papists, in their vows, penances, and satisfactions. I dare say of them (I mean as many of them as act on the principles of their church, as they call it) what Paul says of Israel concerning righteousness (Rom 9:31, 32): they have pursued mortification, but they have not attained it. Why not? “Because their pursuit was not by faith, but as if it were by works.” The same is the state and condition of all among ourselves who, in obedience to their convictions and awakened consciences, try to give up sin. They pursue it, but they do not attain it.

It is true that it #emph[is], and it #emph[will be], required of every person whatever who hears the law or the gospel preached that he mortify sin. It is his #emph[duty], but it is not his #emph[immediate duty]. It is his duty to do it, but to do it in God’s way. Suppose you require your servant to pay a sum of money for you in a certain place, but first to go and collect it in another. It is his duty to pay the money as appointed, and you will blame him if he does not. Yet that was not his immediate duty; he was first to collect it, as you directed. So it is in this case. Sin is to be mortified, but something must be done first to enable us to do it.

1\. I have proved that it is the Spirit alone who can mortify sin. He is promised to do it, and all other means without him are empty and vain. How then shall a man who does not have the Spirit mortify sin? A man could more easily see without eyes or speak without a tongue than truly mortify one sin without the Spirit. Now, how is the Spirit obtained? He is the Spirit of Christ. As the apostle says, “if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, he does not belong to Christ” (Rom 8:9). So if we are Christ’s and have a share in him, we have the Spirit, and only so do we have power for mortification. The apostle argues this at length: “Those controlled by the flesh cannot please God” (Rom 8:8). This is the inference and conclusion he draws from his previous discussion of our natural state and condition, and of the enmity toward God and his law that we have in it. If we are in the flesh, if we do not have the Spirit, we cannot do anything that would please God. But what is our deliverance from this condition? Verse 9: “You#super[+], however, are controlled not by the flesh, but by the Spirit, if the Spirit of God lives in you#super[+]”; that is, “You#super[+] believers, who have the Spirit of Christ, are not in the flesh.” There is no way of deliverance from the state and condition of being in the flesh except by the Spirit of Christ. And what if this Spirit of Christ is in you#super[+]? Why, then you#super[+] are mortified. Verse 10: “your body is dead because of sin,” or to it; mortification is carried on; the new man is made alive to righteousness. The apostle proves this in verse 11 from the union we have with Christ by the Spirit, which will produce workings in us that match what it worked in him. All attempts, then, at mortifying any lust without a share in Christ are vain. Many men are galled with and for sin, when the arrows of Christ for conviction have been made sharp in their hearts by the preaching of the word or by some affliction. They set themselves vigorously against this or that particular lust, the one that has most disquieted or perplexed their consciences. But, poor creatures! they labor in the fire, and their work is burned up. When the Spirit of Christ comes to this work, “He will be like a refiner’s fire, like a launderer’s soap,” and he will purify men like gold and silver (Mal 3:2, 3). He will take away their dross and tin, their filth and blood, as in Isa 4:4. But men must be gold and silver at bottom, or refining will do them no good. The prophet gives us the sad outcome of wicked men’s utmost attempts at mortification, by whatever means God gives them: “The bellows blow fiercely, blasting away the lead with fire. The refining proceeds in vain. … They are called rejected silver, because the #smallcaps[Lord] has rejected them” (Jer 6:29, 30). And what is the reason for this? Verse 28: they were “bronze and iron” when they were put into the furnace. Men may refine bronze and iron for a long time before it will become good silver.

I say, then, that mortification is not the #emph[present] business of #emph[unregenerate] men. God does not call them to it yet. #emph[Conversion] is their work, the conversion of the #emph[whole] soul, not the mortification of #emph[this] or #emph[that particular] lust. You would laugh at a man you saw putting up a great building without taking any care for a foundation; especially if you saw him so foolish that, after a thousand experiences of what he built one day falling down the next, he still kept on in the same course. So it is with convinced persons. They plainly see that the ground they gain against sin one day they lose the next, yet they go on in the same road, without asking where the destructive flaw in their progress lies. When the Jews, convicted of their sin, “were cut to the heart” and cried out, “What shall we do?” (Acts 2:37), what did Peter direct them to do? Did he tell them to go and mortify their pride, wrath, malice, cruelty, and the like? No; he knew that was not their present work. He called them to conversion and faith in Christ in general (verse 38). Let men first be thoroughly converted, and then, as they “look on the One they have pierced,” humiliation and mortification will follow. So when John came preaching repentance and conversion, he said, “The axe lies ready at the root of the trees” (Matt 3:10). The Pharisees had been laying on heavy burdens, imposing tedious duties and rigid means of mortification, in fastings, washings, and the like, all in vain. John says, “The doctrine of conversion is for you#super[+]; the axe in my hand is laid to the root.” And our Savior tells us what is to be done in this case. He says, “Are grapes gathered from thornbushes?” (Matt 7:16). But suppose a thornbush is well pruned and cut, and pains are taken with it? “Yes, but it will never bear figs” (verses 17, 18); every tree must bring forth fruit according to its own kind. What, then, is to be done? He tells us: “Make a tree good and its fruit will be good” (Matt 12:33). The root must be dealt with and the nature of the tree changed, or no good fruit will be brought forth.

This is what I am aiming at: unless a man is #emph[regenerate], unless he is a believer, all the attempts he can make at mortification are to no purpose, however impressive and promising they may be. So are all the means he can use, however diligently, earnestly, watchfully, and intently he follows them in mind and spirit. In vain will he try many remedies; he will not be healed. Yes, several desperate evils attend the attempt to perform this duty by convinced persons who are nothing more than convinced:

(1.) The mind and soul are taken up with what is not the man’s #emph[proper business], and so he is diverted from what is. God lays hold of some sin in him by his word and his judgments. He galls his conscience, disquiets his heart, and deprives him of his rest. Now other diversions will not serve; he must apply himself to the work before him. The business at hand is to awaken the whole man to consider the state and condition he is in, so that he may be brought home to God. Instead, he sets himself to mortify the sin that galls him. This comes purely from self-love, from a wish to be freed from his trouble, and has nothing to do with the work he is called to; and so he is diverted from it. So God tells us of Ephraim, when he says, “I will spread My net over them; I will bring them down like birds of the air. I will chastise them” (Hos 7:12): he caught them, entangled them, and convinced them that they could not escape. Yet he says of them, “They turn, but not to the Most High.” They set themselves to give up sin, but not in the way God called for it, by #emph[universal conversion]. So men are diverted from coming to God by the most glorious ways they can settle on for coming to him. And this is one of the most common deceits by which men ruin their own souls. I wish that some whose trade it is to whitewash flimsy walls in the things of God did not teach this deceit and lead the people astray by their ignorance. What do men do, what are they often directed to do, when their consciences are galled by sin and by disquiet from the Lord, who has laid hold of them? Is not the sum of what they apply themselves to this: to give up in practice the sin whose fruits perplex them, and to make headway against it? And is not the gospel aim of their convictions lost by it? Here men stay and perish.

(2.) This duty is a good thing in itself, in its proper place; it is a duty that gives evidence of sincerity and brings peace home to the conscience. So when a man finds himself really engaged in it, his mind and heart set against this or that sin, with purpose and resolution to have nothing more to do with it, he is ready to conclude that his state and condition are good, and so he deludes his own soul. For:

\[1.\] His conscience has been made sick with sin, and he could find no rest. Then he should go to the great Physician of souls and get healing in his blood. Instead, by this engagement against sin the man pacifies and quiets his conscience, and settles down without going to Christ at all. Ah! how many poor souls are #emph[deluded] like this to eternity! “When Ephraim saw his sickness …, then Ephraim … sent to the great king” (Hos 5:13), and that kept him away from God. The whole bundle of the popish religion is made up of designs and schemes to pacify conscience without Christ, all described by the apostle in Rom 10:3.

\[2.\] By this means men #emph[satisfy] themselves that their state and condition are good, since they are doing a work that is good in itself, and they are not doing it to be seen. They know they want the work done in sincerity, and so they are hardened in a kind of self-righteousness.

(3.) When a man has been deluded like this for a time and has deceived his own soul, he finds over a #emph[long course] of life that his sin is in fact #emph[not mortified], or that if he has changed one sin he has gotten another. Then at length he begins to think that all struggling is in vain and that he will never be able to prevail; he is building a dam against water that keeps rising on him. So #emph[he gives up], as one who despairs of any success, and yields himself to the power of sin and to the habit of formality he has acquired.

And this is the usual outcome for persons who attempt to mortify sin without first obtaining a share in Christ. It #emph[deludes] them, #emph[hardens] them, #emph[destroys] them. That is why we see that there are usually no more vile and desperate sinners in the world than those who were put on this course by conviction, found it fruitless, and abandoned it without discovering Christ. And this is the substance of the religion and godliness of the choicest formalists in the world, and of all those in the Roman synagogue who are driven to mortification, as they drive Indians to baptism or cattle to water. I say, then, that mortification is the work of believers, and of believers only. To kill sin is the work of living men. Where men are #emph[dead] (as all unbelievers, the best of them, are dead), sin is #emph[alive], and will live.

2\. It is the work of #emph[faith], the #emph[special] work of faith. Now, if there is a work to be done that only one instrument can accomplish, it is the greatest madness for anyone who does not have that instrument to try to do it. And it is faith that purifies the heart (Acts 15:9); or, as Peter puts it, we purify our souls “by obedience to the truth through the Spirit” (1 Pet 1:22). Without faith it will not be done.

What has been said is enough, I suppose, to make good my first #emph[general rule]: #emph[Be sure to get a share in Christ; if you intend to mortify any sin without it, it will never be done.]

#emph[Objection.] You#super[+] will say, “What, then, would you have unregenerate men do who are convinced of the evil of sin? Shall they stop striving against sin, live dissolutely, give their lusts free rein, and be as bad as the worst of men? That would be a way to throw the whole world into confusion, to bring everything into darkness, to open the floodgates of lust, and to lay the reins on men’s necks, so that they rush into every sin with delight and greed, like a horse charging into battle.”

#emph[Answer.] 1. God forbid! We must regard it as a great work of the wisdom, goodness, and love of God that by many ways and means he is pleased to restrain the sons of men from running out into all the excess and riot that the depravity of their nature would carry them into with violence. By whatever way this is done, it comes from the care, kindness, and goodness of God; without it the whole earth would be a hell of sin and confusion.

2\. There is a special #emph[convicting] power in the word, which God is often pleased to put forth, to the wounding, astonishing, and in some sense humbling of sinners, even though they are never converted. And the word is to be preached though it has this effect, yet not for this end. So let the word be preached, and men’s sins will be rebuked, lust will be restrained, and some opposition will be made against sin, though that is not the effect aimed at.

3\. Though this is the #emph[work] of the word and the Spirit, and good in itself, yet it is not profitable or effective for the main end in those in whom it is worked. They are still poisoned by bitterness and under the power of darkness.

4\. Let men know that it is their #emph[duty], but in its proper place. I do not take men off mortification; I put them on conversion. The man who calls another away from mending a hole in the wall of his house to put out a fire that is consuming the whole building is not his enemy. Poor soul! It is not your sore finger but your wasting fever that you are to apply yourself to consider. You set yourself against #emph[a particular] sin, and do not consider that you are #emph[nothing] but sin.

Let me add this for those who are preachers of the word, or who intend, by the good hand of God, to take up that work. It is their duty to plead with men about their sins and to lay heavy blows on particular sins. But let them always remember to do it with the proper end of law and gospel in view: that is, to use the sin they speak against to uncover the state and condition the sinner is in. Otherwise, perhaps, they may bring men to formality and hypocrisy, but little of the true end of preaching the gospel will be achieved. It will do no good to beat a man off his drunkenness into a sober formality. A skillful master of the assemblies lays his axe to the root and always drives at the heart. To speak out against the particular sins of ignorant, unregenerate persons, such as the land is full of, is a good work. Yet though it may be done with great efficacy, vigor, and success, if all its effect is that they set themselves to the most diligent efforts to mortify the sins preached against, all that has been done is like beating an enemy in an open field and driving him into an impregnable castle, where he cannot be defeated. Do you at any time have a sinner at an advantage, on account of any one sin whatever? Do you have anything to take hold of him by? Bring it to his state and condition, drive it home to the head, and deal with him there. To break men off particular sins and not to break their hearts is to rob ourselves of our advantages for dealing with them.

And here the Roman mortification is grievously at fault. They drive all sorts of persons to it, without the least consideration of whether they have a principle for it or not. Yes, they are so far from calling on men to believe, so that they may be able to mortify their lusts, that they call men to mortification instead of believing. The truth is, they know neither what it is to #emph[believe] nor what #emph[mortification] itself means. Faith with them is only a general assent to the doctrine taught in their church; and mortification is a man’s binding himself by a vow to some fixed course of life in which he denies himself some of the use of the things of this world, not without a considerable compensation. Such men know neither the Scriptures nor the power of God. Their boasting of their mortification is only glorying in their shame. Some casuists among ourselves overlook the necessity of regeneration and openly give this as a direction to all sorts of persons who complain of any sin or lust: that they should vow against it, at least for a time, a month or so. They seem to have a scrap of light in the mystery of the gospel, much like that of Nicodemus when he first came to Christ. They tell men to vow to abstain from their sin for a time. This commonly makes their lust more violent. Perhaps with great perplexity they keep their word; perhaps not, which increases their guilt and torment. Is their sin mortified at all by this? Do they find a conquest over it? Is their condition changed, even if they manage to give it up? Are they not still poisoned by bitterness? Is this not to make men make bricks, if not without #emph[straw], then, which is worse, without #emph[strength]? What promise does any unregenerate man have to support him in this work? What help to perform it? Can sin be killed without a share in the death of Christ, or mortified without the Spirit? If such directions should succeed in changing men’s lives, as they seldom do, they still never reach the change of their hearts or of their condition. They may make men self-justifiers or hypocrites, not Christians. It often grieves me to see poor souls who have a zeal for God and a desire for their eternal welfare kept all their days by such directors and directions under a hard, burdensome, outward worship and service of God, with many impressive efforts at mortification, in utter ignorance of the righteousness of Christ and unacquainted with his Spirit. I know too many persons and things of this kind. If God ever shines into their hearts to give them the knowledge of his glory in the face of his Son Jesus Christ, they will see the folly of their present way.
